\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Literature context of this release's thresholds, in total power (see text; the earlier W\,Hz$^{-1}$ panel is withdrawn). Blue: this survey, deepest window per star (88{} stars, 1.30--39.63\,pc), $1.6\times 10^{13}$--$1.5\times 10^{16}$\,W, median $5.6\times 10^{14}$\,W. Orange diamond: \citet  {Enriquez2017}, ${\sim }10^{13}$\,W within 50\,pc. Aqua squares: \citet  {Margot2023}, $1.35\times 10^{13}$\,W within 100\,pc and $5.08\times 10^{16}$\,W within 6135\,pc. Violet triangle: \citet  {Mason2024}, archival ALMA Band\nobreakspace  {}3, $6.91\times 10^{17}$\,W for their closest star at 1.01\,kpc. Left-edge ticks: deepest per-target limits of \citet  {Price2020}, $2\times 10^{12}$\,W (GBT) and $9\times 10^{12}$\,W (Parkes), no single sample distance. Grey dotted: EIRP $\propto d^{2}$ at constant received flux, normalised to $4\times 10^{12}$\,W at 1\,pc, a guide not a fit. Green dashed: an Arecibo-like planetary radar ($2\times 10^{13}$\,W), purely a technological power scale; only our G\nobreakspace  {}272-61, in its two resolved components, falls below it. Thresholds, drift coverage and confidence conventions differ among these surveys: context, not a ranking.}}{3}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:context}{{1}{3}{Literature context of this release's thresholds, in total power (see text; the earlier W\,Hz$^{-1}$ panel is withdrawn). Blue: this survey, deepest window per star (\NStars {} stars, \DistMin --\DistMax \,pc), $1.6\times 10^{13}$--$1.5\times 10^{16}$\,W, median $5.6\times 10^{14}$\,W. Orange diamond: \citet {Enriquez2017}, ${\sim }10^{13}$\,W within 50\,pc. Aqua squares: \citet {Margot2023}, $1.35\times 10^{13}$\,W within 100\,pc and $5.08\times 10^{16}$\,W within 6135\,pc. Violet triangle: \citet {Mason2024}, archival ALMA Band~3, $6.91\times 10^{17}$\,W for their closest star at 1.01\,kpc. Left-edge ticks: deepest per-target limits of \citet {Price2020}, $2\times 10^{12}$\,W (GBT) and $9\times 10^{12}$\,W (Parkes), no single sample distance. Grey dotted: EIRP $\propto d^{2}$ at constant received flux, normalised to $4\times 10^{12}$\,W at 1\,pc, a guide not a fit. Green dashed: an Arecibo-like planetary radar ($2\times 10^{13}$\,W), purely a technological power scale; only our G~272-61, in its two resolved components, falls below it. Thresholds, drift coverage and confidence conventions differ among these surveys: context, not a ranking}{figure.1}{}}
