\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces The four windows containing a control-ring exceedance and the evidence dispositioning each. $T_\star $ is the search statistic at the stellar position (Eq.\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {eq:tstar}), `ring max' the largest of that window's 512{} control statistics. $\Delta \nu $ and $\Delta v$ are the same offset of the crossing from the nearest of the eight masked species' catalogued transitions, in frequency and in velocity, topocentric; the $\pm 50$\,km\,s$^{-1}$ mask applies in the stellar frame after the conversion chain of Appendix\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {app:conventions}. Giving both makes the conversion explicit for every row rather than for $\beta $\nobreakspace  {}Pic alone: at these window frequencies 1\,MHz is 2.6, 1.3 and 0.87\,km\,s$^{-1}$ in Bands\nobreakspace  {}3, 6 and\nobreakspace  {}7 respectively. No disposition rests on a single test.}}{11}{table.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:flagged}{{5}{11}{The four windows containing a control-ring exceedance and the evidence dispositioning each. $T_\star $ is the search statistic at the stellar position (Eq.~\ref {eq:tstar}), `ring max' the largest of that window's \NCtrl {} control statistics. $\Delta \nu $ and $\Delta v$ are the same offset of the crossing from the nearest of the eight masked species' catalogued transitions, in frequency and in velocity, topocentric; the $\pm 50$\,km\,s$^{-1}$ mask applies in the stellar frame after the conversion chain of Appendix~\ref {app:conventions}. Giving both makes the conversion explicit for every row rather than for $\beta $~Pic alone: at these window frequencies 1\,MHz is 2.6, 1.3 and 0.87\,km\,s$^{-1}$ in Bands~3, 6 and~7 respectively. No disposition rests on a single test}{table.5}{}}
