\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Every window flagged by the original region-max statistic, shown under both statistics, so the effect of the revision can be audited across all of them rather than taken on trust for one target. $S_{\rm  reg}$ is the maximum of Eq.\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {eq:tstar} over the $n_{\rm  src}=135$-position source region, `ctrl max' the largest of the 512{} single-position controls it was compared with; $T_\star $ is the symmetric single-position statistic at the propagated stellar position and `ring max' the largest of the same window's 512{} control statistics. $\nu $ is the window centre. The symmetric criterion flags a strict subset, four of the seven. In the three that drop out the stellar position never exceeded its own control ring: the region-max statistic cleared the ring only by maximising over the source region. For CP$-$72\nobreakspace  {}2713 the region maximum already lay on the star, so the two statistics coincide exactly.}}{16}{table.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:bothstats}{{8}{16}{Every window flagged by the original region-max statistic, shown under both statistics, so the effect of the revision can be audited across all of them rather than taken on trust for one target. $S_{\rm reg}$ is the maximum of Eq.~\ref {eq:tstar} over the $n_{\rm src}=135$-position source region, `ctrl max' the largest of the \NCtrl {} single-position controls it was compared with; $T_\star $ is the symmetric single-position statistic at the propagated stellar position and `ring max' the largest of the same window's \NCtrl {} control statistics. $\nu $ is the window centre. The symmetric criterion flags a strict subset, four of the seven. In the three that drop out the stellar position never exceeded its own control ring: the region-max statistic cleared the ring only by maximising over the source region. For CP$-$72~2713 the region maximum already lay on the star, so the two statistics coincide exactly}{table.8}{}}
